WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.054
An AI agent with no memory hallucinates.

00:00:07.054 --> 00:00:12.108
That is panel one: the model alone, with only a context window, which is wiped when the session ends.

00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:17.163
Panel two: give it a local database, a file on disk, and it looks the answer up instead.

00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:22.217
Panel three: scale that same database across machines, and it holds one private memory for each of eight hundred million users.

00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:27.271
No-memory, then local, then global.

00:00:27.271 --> 00:00:32.325
That arc is the spine of this whole course.

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:35.576
Start with the failure.

00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:38.827
Ask an agent about a function you wrote last week.

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:42.078
With no memory, it does not look it up.

00:00:42.078 --> 00:00:45.328
It guesses.

00:00:45.328 --> 00:00:48.579
A plausible name, a plausible signature, and it is wrong.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:51.830
The context window held the real answer, but the window is gone.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.081
That is a hallucination, and it is the default state of a model that remembers nothing.

00:00:55.081 --> 00:00:58.750
Why not just keep everything in the context window?

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.419
Because the window is small and temporary.

00:01:02.419 --> 00:01:06.088
It is working memory, and it resets every session.

00:01:06.088 --> 00:01:09.756
Anything that has to outlast the session, your preferences, past decisions, the shape of your codebase, cannot live there.

00:01:09.756 --> 00:01:13.425
It has to live somewhere durable.

00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:17.094
It has to live in a database.

00:01:17.094 --> 00:01:23.001
Local memory is the cure, and it is just SQL.

00:01:23.001 --> 00:01:28.908
The model writes facts to an embedded database with a SQL write, and reads them back with a SQL read, through a small rerank step.

00:01:28.908 --> 00:01:34.816
The database, claude-mem, is one file on your machine.

00:01:34.816 --> 00:01:40.723
It holds two indexes: a keyword index for what you said, and a vector index for what you meant.

00:01:40.723 --> 00:01:46.630
One file, no server, and the agent stops guessing.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.119
Now scale it.

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:53.608
ChatGPT has eight hundred million users, each needing their own private memory.

00:01:53.608 --> 00:01:57.096
The pattern does not change, only the size.

00:01:57.096 --> 00:02:00.585
Every user's writes go to one Postgres primary.

00:02:00.585 --> 00:02:04.074
The primary streams its changes to about fifty read replicas.

00:02:04.074 --> 00:02:07.562
And all those reads fan out across the replicas.

00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:11.051
Same SQL write, same SQL read.

00:02:11.051 --> 00:02:14.540
One machine becomes many.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.344
One idea, twice.

00:02:17.344 --> 00:02:20.147
A database gives a model persistent memory.

00:02:20.147 --> 00:02:22.951
Local, it is a single file.

00:02:22.951 --> 00:02:25.755
Global, it is Postgres across many machines.

00:02:25.755 --> 00:02:28.559
The model keeps changing; the database is the constant infrastructure underneath.

00:02:28.559 --> 00:02:31.362
Local memory is Modules one to four.

00:02:31.362 --> 00:02:34.166
Global memory is Modules five and six.

00:02:34.166 --> 00:02:36.970
That is the course.
